  "body": "Hey guys,\r\n\r\nRecently, I have been experimenting with your API (chrome 83.0.4103.116). The API is easy to work with, and connecting the device to my MacBook Pro (15-inch, 2017, Mojave) is a breeze. However, I'm having some trouble reading (terminating) streams. I'm experimenting with a medical device, which has a USB interface and returns JSON.\r\n\r\n```js\r\nlet reader;\r\nlet writer;\r\nconst encoder = new TextEncoder();\r\nconst decoder = new TextDecoder();\r\n\r\nasync function connect() {\r\n  const port = await navigator.serial.requestPort();\r\n  await port.open({ baudrate: 9600 });\r\n  reader = port.readable.getReader();\r\n  writer = port.writable.getWriter();\r\n}\r\n\r\nasync function config() {\r\n  await writer.write(encoder.encode(\"configuration get\\n\"));\r\n  while (true) {\r\n    console.log(\"Start reading chunk\")\r\n    const { value, done } = await reader.read();\r\n    console.log(done)\r\n    if (done) {\r\n      break;\r\n    }\r\n    const data = decoder.decode(value);\r\n    console.log(data);\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport default {\r\n  connect,\r\n  config,\r\n};\r\n```\r\nThe snippet above is what I use to communicate with my device. Writing seems to work fine. When reading, however, the loop doesn't break, because \"done\" is never true. The last chunk of data contains (at the end) a null terminator + 5 byte termination (\\0\\r\\n\\r\\n>). After the last chunk of data - with termination - is received; the reader is called again and freezes. \r\n\r\nAny idea how I can fix this issue?\r\n",
